# 🧵 Secure Coding Series #5: Identity and Session Management – Keeping Track of Who’s Who

## 👥 What Is Identity and Session Management?

This topic covers two essential ideas:

- **Identity management**: Creating, verifying, and managing users' identities
- **Session management**: Keeping track of a logged-in user’s identity during their time on the site or app

Both are **fundamental to security**. If you can’t properly manage “who is who” and “who is still online”, you expose yourself to:

- Session hijacking
- Privilege escalation
- Unauthorized access to sensitive actions

---

## 🧨 Common Vulnerabilities

| Problem                         | Description                                       |
|---------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| Insecure session IDs            | Predictable or reused session tokens              |
| No session expiration           | Sessions remain active indefinitely               |
| No logout / invalidation        | Users can't truly log out                         |
| Session fixation                | Attacker sets session ID before login             |
| Weak session cookie settings    | No `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, or `SameSite` flags      |

---

## ✅ Best Practices for Identity Management

- Assign a **unique identifier (UUID)** for each user
- Never expose internal user IDs (like DB row IDs) in URLs
- Use secure, centralized identity providers (OAuth, SSO) when possible
- Enforce **email verification**, **password reset tokens**, and **MFA**
- Log identity-related events: logins, role changes, failed attempts

---

## ✅ Best Practices for Session Management

- Use **random, long session tokens**
- Store session tokens only in **HttpOnly** cookies
- Set cookie attributes:
  - `Secure`: only over HTTPS
  - `HttpOnly`: inaccessible from JS
  - `SameSite=Strict`: block cross-origin leaks
- Rotate session tokens after login or privilege change
- **Invalidate session** on logout or timeout (e.g., 15–30 min idle)

---

## 🧑‍💻 Example: ASP.NET Core Cookie Settings

(csharp)
services.ConfigureApplicationCookie(options =>
{
    options.Cookie.HttpOnly = true;
    options.Cookie.SecurePolicy = CookieSecurePolicy.Always;
    options.Cookie.SameSite = SameSiteMode.Strict;
    options.ExpireTimeSpan = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(30);
    options.SlidingExpiration = true;
});

✅ This setup ensures:
	•	The cookie is sent over HTTPS only
	•	Cannot be accessed by client-side scripts
	•	Will expire after 30 mins of inactivity

🔁 Session Rotation Example (on login)

(C#)
await HttpContext.SignOutAsync();
await HttpContext.SignInAsync(userPrincipal);

This kills any old session and starts a new one — preventing session fixation.

(Node.js - javascript)

app.use(session({
  secret: process.env.SESSION_SECRET,
  resave: false,
  saveUninitialized: false,
  cookie: {
    httpOnly: true,
    secure: true,
    sameSite: "strict",
    maxAge: 30 * 60 * 1000 // 30 minutes
  }
}));

